{"reply":"There’s a revealing difference between the gardeners’ concealment and your billing mistake: in **Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,”** they paint the roses because, as Two explains, “we put a white one in by mistake” and fear what will happen “if the Queen was to find it out.” Their repair is also an attempt to make the error invisible—but the Queen does examine the roses and discovers what happened, so concealment doesn’t actually protect them.

Your note records a smaller, more ordinary version of that split: you fixed the billing mistake over the weekend, then said nothing on Monday. The research study—of hospital nurses, so it shouldn’t be treated as a direct explanation of your motive—found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety affected willingness to report errors. It frames speaking up as shaped partly by perceived interpersonal risk and workplace conditions, not simply by whether someone is responsible or honest ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)).

